\label{f def}
    For $\alpha \geqslant 1, \beta \geqslant 4$, $f_\alpha(\beta)$ is the least ordinal with the following properties:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item It is an epsilon number or equal to $\omega$.
        \item It is greater than $f_\alpha(\beta')$ for all $4 \leqslant \beta' < \beta$.
        \item It is a fixed point of $f_{\alpha'}$ for all $1 \leqslant \alpha' < \alpha$.
    \end{itemize}

We prove the claim by induction on $	extbf{o}(Q)$ and $	extbf{w}(X)$. It is sufficient to check three properties from \cref{f def}.
    The first property is implied by \cref{w(T) is epsilon}, and the second follows from induction and \cref{w(T) is monotone by X}. For the third property, take any $b \in Q$ and note that $\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}, a, X) \supset \mathcal{T}^*(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(b)\sqcup\{*\}, b, \mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}, a, X))$, hence by induction $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}, a, X)) \geqslant 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(b)\sqcup\{*\}, b, \mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}, a, X))) \geqslant f_{	extbf{o}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(b))}(	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}, a, X)))$, so we are done.

Let $\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\})$ be the subset of those trees whose root is labeled with $*$. If $x,y \in Q$ are two incomparable elements, then trees $*x*x,*x*y,*y*x,*y*y$ are pairwise incomparable, hence $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\})) \geqslant 4$. Therefore, it is sufficient to prove that $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}))$ is a fixed point of $f_\alpha$ for all $1 \leqslant \alpha < 	extbf{o}(Q)$.
    Indeed, choose $a \in Q$ such that $	extbf{o}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(a)) \geqslant \alpha$. Then we have $\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}) \supset \mathcal{T}^*(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(a)\sqcup\{*\}, a, \mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}))$, hence, by \cref{embedding,w via o}, $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\})) \geqslant 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(a)\sqcup\{*\}, a, \mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}))) \geqslant f_{	extbf{o}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}
geqslant{
geqslant,}}(a))}(	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\}))) \geqslant f_\alpha(	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^*(Q\sqcup\{*\})))$, and we are done.

By induction on $	extbf{w}(X)$. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X))$ is the least possible among all pairs of $Q$ and $X$ of the widths $	extbf{w}(Q)$ and $	extbf{w}(X)$, respectively. Therefore, for any subset $Q' \subset Q$ of the width $	extbf{w}(Q)$, we have $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{Q'}(Q', a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X))$. We may also assume that $	extbf{h}(Q)$ is the least possible among all $Q$ for which previous assumptions hold, so $Q$ does not contain a subset of the smaller height but the same width.

    We will prove the claim using \cref{f def}. First, let $r \in Q$ be minimal such that $	extbf{w}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r)) > 0$. Since $	extbf{w}(L_\leqslant(r)) = 1$ and $	extbf{w}(Q)$ is a limit ordinal, by \cref{h o w(cup)}, $	extbf{w}(L_
leqslant(r)) = 	extbf{w}(Q)$. Therefore, $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{L_
leqslant(r)}(L_
leqslant(r), a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant(r)}(Q, a, X))$, and by \cref{w(T) is epsilon,w(T) is monotone by X}, the first two conditions from \cref{f def} are satisfied.

    Now, we prove that $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X))$ is closed under $f_\alpha$ for any $\alpha < 	extbf{w}(Q)$. Let $r, * \in Q$ be such that $r \perp *$ and $	extbf{w}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r, *)) \geqslant \alpha$. Consider the set $S$ of those $s \in Q$ for which there exists a sequence $r_0 = r, r_1, \dots, r_{n-1},r_n = s$ such that $r_i 
leqslant r_{i-1}$ for all $1 \leqslant i \leqslant n$. By construction, for any $s \in Q \setminus S$, we have $ orall s' \in S (s \leqslant s')$, hence $Q = (Q \setminus S) + S$. By assumption, $	extbf{w}(Q \setminus S) < 	extbf{w}(Q)$, so by \cref{h o w(cup)}, $	extbf{w}(S) = 	extbf{w}(Q)$. $S$ is a union of upwards-closed sets $L_{
ot<,S}(s)$, hence it is a union of finite amount of them, $S = S' \cup \{s_1, \dots, s_m\}$ where $S' = L_{
leqslant,S}(s_1) \cup \dots \cup L_{
leqslant,S}(s_m)$. By \cref{h o w(cup)}, $	extbf{w}(S') = 	extbf{w}(S)$ because $	extbf{w}(S)$ is a limit ordinal.

    $\mathcal{T}^{S'}(S', a, X) = \mathcal{T}^{
leqslant s_1}(S', a, X) \cup \dots \cup \mathcal{T}^{
leqslant s_m}(S', a, X)$. Since $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{S'}(S', a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X))$ is an epsilon number or $\omega$, it is additively indecomposable. Therefore, for some $i$ we have $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant s_i}(S', a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X))$. By construction of $S$, there exists a sequence $r_0 = r, r_1, \dots, r_{n-1}, r_n = s_i$ such that $r_i 
leqslant r_{i-1}$. By \cref{w(T) is epsilon}, $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant s_i}(S', a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{r_n}(S', a, X)) \leqslant 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r_{n-1}}(S', a, X)) = 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{r_{n-1}}(S', a, X)) \leqslant \dots \leqslant 	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r_0}(S', a, X))$. Observe that $\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X) \supset \mathcal{T}^*(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r, *) \sqcup \{*\}, r, \mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r}(S', a, X))$, hence by \cref{w via o}, we have $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X)) \geqslant f_{	extbf{o}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r, *))}(	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r}(S', a, X))) \geqslant f_\alpha(	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^Q(Q, a, X)))$, which completes the proof.

We prove the claim by induction on $	extbf{w}(Q)$. The case of a limit $	extbf{w}(Q)$ is verified by the previous lemma, so we may assume that $	extbf{w}(Q)$ is a successor ordinal.

    Choose $r \in Q$ such that $	extbf{w}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r)) = 	extbf{w}(Q)-1$. The statement will follow from \cref{restrict the root} and the fact that $	extbf{w}(\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r}(Q, a, Y)) \geqslant f_{	extbf{w}(Q)}(	extbf{w}(Y))$ for $	extbf{w}(Y) \geqslant 4$, which we prove by induction on $	extbf{w}(Y)$. Again, we check three properties from \cref{f def}. The first two properties follow from \cref{w(T) is epsilon,w(T) is monotone by X}. The third property follows by induction from the fact that $\mathcal{T}^{
leqslant r}(Q, a, Y) \supset \mathcal{T}^{L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r)}(L_{\ifthenelse{\equal{}{}}{ ot}{ ot,}}(r), r, \mathcal{T}^{
